    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nThe repository pattern isolates persistence access behind a domain-oriented interface so business logic is less coupled to a specific database/client.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nStart with one small example, identify the compile-time contract, then trace the runtime JavaScript behavior.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ninterface UserRepository {\n  getById(\n    id: string,\n  ): Promise<User>;\n}\n\ntype User = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n};\n\nclass MemoryUserRepository\n  implements UserRepository {\n  private readonly users = [\n    { id: \"1\", name: \"Ada\" },\n  ];\n\n  async getById(\n    id: string,\n  ): Promise<User> {\n    const user = this.users.find(\n      value => value.id === id,\n    );\n\n    if (!user) {\n      throw new Error(\"Not found\");\n    }\n\n    return user;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / IDIOMATIC TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst user =\n  await userRepository.getById(\"1\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\n{ id: \"1\", name: \"Ada\" }\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nDo not create repositories that merely mirror every underlying ORM method; expose useful domain operations.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck null/undefined, invalid external data, inference loss, module/runtime boundaries, errors, stale async work, and API evolution where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain what the compiler proves, what exists at runtime, why the idiomatic/built-in approach is useful, and the maintainability/performance trade-off.",
