    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nObserver/Pub-Sub decouples event producers from subscribers and is useful for cross-cutting notifications or event-driven workflows.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nStart with one small example, identify the compile-time contract, then trace the runtime JavaScript behavior.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype Listener = (\n  event: string,\n) => void;\n\nconst listeners: Listener[] = [];\n\nfunction subscribe(\n  listener: Listener,\n): () => void {\n  listeners.push(listener);\n\n  return () => {\n    const index =\n      listeners.indexOf(listener);\n\n    if (index >= 0) {\n      listeners.splice(index, 1);\n    }\n  };\n}\n\nconst unsubscribe =\n  subscribe(console.log);\n\nfor (const listener of listeners) {\n  listener(\"ORDER_CREATED\");\n}\n\nunsubscribe();\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / IDIOMATIC TypeScript:\n```ts\neventBus.on(\n  \"ORDER_CREATED\",\n  handleOrderCreated,\n);\n\neventBus.off(\n  \"ORDER_CREATED\",\n  handleOrderCreated,\n);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\nORDER_CREATED\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nAlways define subscription cleanup and event contracts.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck null/undefined, invalid external data, inference loss, module/runtime boundaries, errors, stale async work, and API evolution where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain what the compiler proves, what exists at runtime, why the idiomatic/built-in approach is useful, and the maintainability/performance trade-off.",
